      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Work-related musculoskeletal pain is a major occupational problem. Those with pain in multiple sites usually report worse health outcomes than those with pain in one site."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This study explored prevalence and associated predictors of multi-site pain in health care sector employees."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Survey responses from 1348 health care sector employees across three organisations (37% response rate) collected data on job satisfaction, work life balance, psychosocial and physical hazards, general health and work ability. Musculoskeletal discomfort was measured across 5 body regions with pain in ≥ 2 sites defined as multi-site pain. Generalized linear models were used to identify relationships between work-related factors and multi-site pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Over 52% of the employees reported pain in multiple body sites and 19% reported pain in one site. Poor work life balance (PRR\u200a=\u200a2.33, 95% CI\u200a=\u200a1.06-5.14). physical (PRR\u200a=\u200a7.58, 95% CI\u200a=\u200a4.89-11.77) and psychosocial (PRR\u200a=\u200a1.59, 95% CI\u200a=\u200a1.00-2.57) hazard variables were related to multi-site pain (after controlling for age, gender, health and work ability. Older employees and females were more likely to report multi-site pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Effective risk management of work related multi-site pain must include identification and control of psychosocial and physical hazards."
        }
      ]},
